 "dinosaurs": {
  "find": "From Hintze Hall, take the corridor on the left side of the hall, towards the Darwin Centre. The Dinosaurs gallery is the third doorway on the left. At busy times there is a queue. Count the doorways: one, two, dinosaurs.",
  "arrive": "You are there when a raised walkway carries you up among dinosaur skeletons.",
  "look": [
   "The walkway. It carries you above the floor, at the height of the skeletons. Eye contact is allowed.",
   "The Triceratops skull: find the bony frill, then count the horns. Three. Hence the name.",
   "The moving T. rex. A model, not a skeleton. Follow the noise: it is the loudest thing here."
  ],
  "story": [
   "Step onto the walkway. It lifts you up among the skeletons, so that you meet them almost eye to eye. Try not to blink first. This is one of the busiest rooms in the museum, so there is no prize for speed. Go at the pace of the crowd. And while you do, don't look at the dinosaurs yet. Look at the people looking at them. Nobody walks in here bored. Look down, too: from up here the gallery floor is a slow river of heads.",
   "As you shuffle along, a word about the man who started all this. Richard Owen, who founded this museum, named the dinosaurs, but he imagined them as something like giant scaly rhinoceroses, plodding about on four sturdy legs. In the 1850s, with his advice, life-size models were made for Crystal Palace Park in south London, and they are still there, standing about on all fours and looking very pleased with themselves. Some dinosaurs did walk on four legs. Many walked on two. Science got there in the end. It usually does, eventually, and with a great deal of arguing.",
   "Walk on a little, and look at the bones nearest you. Very few dinosaurs are ever found whole. When an animal dies it is usually eaten, scattered, or simply rots away. Only rarely is it buried fast, in mud or sand, where over millions of years minerals seep into the bone and slowly turn it to stone. So most of the skeletons you see in museums are a careful mixture of real bone, casts and the best judgement of scientists: a jigsaw, with some of the pieces made to order. Bones are not the only clues, either. Scars on a bone show where muscles were attached, and fossil footprints show how an animal walked, and how fast. For a few small feathered dinosaurs, scientists have even found the microscopic structures that once held colour, and one of them turns out to have had a ginger and white striped tail. A dinosaur in a football scarf, more or less.",
   "Keep going, and try to hold the time in your head. Dinosaurs first appeared about two hundred and thirty million years ago, and the great ones vanished about sixty-six million years ago. That is a reign of more than a hundred and sixty million years. Our own species has been around for about three hundred thousand. If the dinosaurs had the whole of a day, we have had roughly the last three minutes before midnight, and in that time we have built a museum and a gift shop.",
   "Now find the Triceratops skull. You are looking for a great frill of bone, flaring out behind the head like an enormous collar, and three horns: two long ones above the eyes and a short one on the nose. That is the name, Greek for three-horned face. At the front there is a beak, rather like a parrot's, and behind it rows of teeth made for slicing through tough plants. It lived at the very end of the age of dinosaurs, in what is now western North America. A plant eater. A very large, very well-equipped plant eater. Triceratops had one of the largest heads of any land animal that has ever lived, which is worth remembering the next time someone tells you that you have a big head.",
   "Stay with the skull a moment. When the first Triceratops horns turned up in the American West, in the 1880s, the palaeontologist Othniel Charles Marsh decided they belonged to an extinct giant bison. An honest mistake, if you squint. He soon corrected himself and gave the animal the name it has now. What the frill was for is still argued over: defence, display, a way of telling its own kind apart. Probably some of each. Scientists change their minds about dinosaurs all the time. It is one of the nicer things about them. Marsh, by the way, spent years locked in a feud with another fossil hunter, Edward Drinker Cope, known as the Bone Wars, in which the two men spied on each other, bribed each other's diggers and badmouthed each other across the American West. Between them they named well over a hundred new kinds of dinosaur. Petty for them. Excellent for us.",
   "Triceratops lived alongside Tyrannosaurus rex. Imagine the neighbours. Then again, we don't have to imagine entirely. Triceratops bones have been found scored with the tooth marks of a tyrannosaur, and at least one of those wounds had healed. Somewhere, sixty-something million years ago, a Triceratops had a very bad day, and lived.",
   "Speaking of which, follow the noise. The loudest thing in the gallery is the T. rex, and it is not a skeleton at all but a moving model, made to show the animal as it might have been in life. When you reach it, don't look at the dinosaur first. Look at the children closest to it. Then at the grown-ups just behind, pretending they only came for the children. Do not try for the front. The front belongs to the children, and they are not giving it up.",
   "While you wait your turn, the real animal. Tyrannosaurus rex means tyrant lizard king, and it was named in 1905, from bones found in Montana. The biggest known were about twelve metres long, with teeth the size of bananas and one of the strongest bites of any land animal we know of. Then there are the arms: short, with just two fingers each, and the subject of jokes ever since. Nobody is quite sure what they were for. What it sounded like, nobody knows either. Its closest living relatives are crocodiles and birds, so the model's roar is an educated guess, made with feeling. Its eyes faced forward, too, so it could judge distance rather well. Bear that in mind.",
   "One last thing, as you head out. The age of dinosaurs ended about sixty-six million years ago, when an asteroid about ten kilometres across struck the Earth in what is now Mexico. Dust and soot darkened the skies, plants withered, and around three quarters of all species on Earth died out. But not every dinosaur went. One small, feathered group survived, and their descendants are flying about outside this building right now. Birds are dinosaurs. So the next time a pigeon in South Kensington looks at you sideways, show some respect. And keep a little wonder in reserve: later on this walk, in Earth Hall, you will meet the most complete skeleton of her kind ever found. No spoilers."
  ],
  "walking": [
   "This museum has been in the dinosaur business since before it opened. Its founder, Richard Owen, coined the word dinosaur in the 1840s, from the Greek for terrible lizard. He had only a handful of bones and teeth to go on, all found in England, and he guessed, correctly, that they belonged to a group of animals nobody had named before.",
   "He guessed wrong about what they looked like, as we will see. But the name stuck, and the queue you may be about to join is, in a sense, his fault."
  ],
  "tip": "One of the busiest galleries in the museum, with a queue at busy times. The museum says weekday afternoons are its quietest."
 },
